#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163281 :tutorial/content "The joint PMF $f(x,y)$ of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ tells us all possible events $(X, Y)$ and the probabilities associated with each event. However, suppose we wish to determine the probability mass function for $X$ only. How can this be deduced from the joint PMF?\n\nLet's build some intuition by considering an example.\n\nConsider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X={\\color{blue}{1}}$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X={\\color{red}{2}}$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nThe support of $X$ is $S_X = \\{{\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{2}}\\}.$ Let's now deduce the probabilities associated with each value of $X$ in $S_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- According to the law of total probability, we can deduce $P(X=1)$ by summing the values in the first row of the table, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =P(X=1,Y=1)+P(X=1,Y=2) \\\\  & =0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we summed over all possible values of $Y$ associated with $X=1.$\n\n- Similarly, by summing the values in the second row in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =P(X=2,Y=1)+P(X=2,Y=2) \\\\  & =0.7+0.1 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we summed over all possible values of $Y$ associated with $X=2.$\n\nTherefore, the probability mass function for $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n\nNotice that we have used the notation $f_X(x).$ Since $f_X(x)$ was deduced from the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ we call $f_X(x)$ the **marginal mass function** for $X.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0281a245-2f6d-5a89-8ea7-5d77bf3dca4f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5469 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163331 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to the last example for the random variables $X$ and $Y.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe saw that the marginal mass function $f_X$ for $X$ is found by summing the rows in the table. So let's add the sum of each row to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.2}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.8}$ |\n\nNotice how we wrote the values of the *marginal* mass function in the right *margin* of the table.\n\nFrom here, we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\nP(X=1) = f_X(1) = 0.2, \\qquad P(X=2) = f_X(2) = 0.8.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can compute the marginal mass function of $Y,$ denoted $f_Y(y),$ by summing the columns. Adding these totals to our table, we get the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y={\\color{blue}{1}}$ | $Y={\\color{red}{2}}$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.8$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.75}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ | $1$ |\n\nAgain, notice how we wrote the values of the *marginal* mass function in the bottom *margin* of the table.\n\nFrom here, we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\nP(Y={\\color{blue}{1}}) = f_Y({\\color{blue}{1}}) = 0.75, \\qquad P(Y={\\color{red}{2}}) = f_Y({\\color{red}{2}}) = 0.25.\n$$\n\nFinally, the marginal mass function $f_Y(y)$ of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1c9ecf8-619b-58b7-899f-b179dca8ae45" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6290 :tutorial/title "Deducing the Marginal Mass Function for Y"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163332 :tutorial/content "Now that we've built some intuition, let's define the marginal mass functions formally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be the joint PMF of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively.\n\n- The **marginal mass function ** of $X,$ denoted $f_X(x),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = P(X=x)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{y \\in S_Y} f(x,y).\n$$\n\n- The **marginal mass function ** of $Y,$ denoted $f_Y(y),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_Y(y)= P(Y=y)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S_X} f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe can relate these definitions to our previous intuition:\n\n- The first definition states that, to compute $P(X=x)$ for some particular value $x,$ we sum all possible values of $Y$ in the row corresponding to $X=x.$\n\n- The second definition states that, to compute $P(Y=y)$ for some particular value $y,$ we sum all possible values of $X$ in the column corresponding to $Y=y.$\n\nFinally, note that marginal mass functions are sometimes called **marginal probability mass functions**, **marginal PMFs**, or (less formally) **marginal distributions**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ddca7e0f-a315-5567-97d7-f5b40876617b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6291 :tutorial/title "A Formal Definition of the Marginal Mass Function"}]}
